            "captions": {
                "instagram": "There's an old joke about how many angels can dance on the head of a pin that I've always found deeply offensive\u2014not because it's stupid, but because it's almost profound. You take something vast (heaven), cram it into something absurdly small (a pinhead), and what do you get? A theological argument that makes people want to throw furniture at medieval scholars.\n\nBut here's where Wigman is smarter than those theologians: he doesn't claim the microcosm equals the macrocosm. He just asks you to look. And once you start looking\u2014really looking\u2014the whole metaphor collapses under its own weight in the most beautiful way possible.\n\nTake your marriage. Your actual, living, breathing marriage\u2014or if you're me, the concept of one. There's this tiny ritual my partner has when she folds laundry. She creases every shirt with military precision while watching reality TV, and there's something in that gesture that contains the entire history of human ambition: care so precise it borders on devotion, paired with entertainment so mindless it borders on nihilism. Two opposite impulses holding hands across a basket of socks. That's not just my relationship. That's every civilization ever built by creatures who simultaneously wanted to make things perfect...\n\nThe next chapter is waiting. The Nine Stitches is available now.\n\n#TheNineStitches #BookWritingJourney #WritingWisdom #CreativeProcess",
                "linkedin": "There's an old joke about how many angels can dance on the head of a pin that I've always found deeply offensive\u2014not because it's stupid, but because it's almost profound. You take something vast (heaven), cram it into something absurdly small (a pinhead), and what do you get? A theological argument that makes people want to throw furniture at medieval scholars.\n\nBut here's where Wigman is smarter than those theologians: he doesn't claim the microcosm equals the macrocosm. He just asks you to look. And once you start looking\u2014really looking\u2014the whole metaphor collapses under its own weight in the most beautiful way possible.\n\nTake your marriage. Your actual, living, breathing marriage\u2014or if you're me, the concept of one. There's this tiny ritual my partner has when she folds laundry. She creases every shirt with military precision while watching reality TV, and there's something in that gesture that contains the entire history of human ambition: care so precise it borders on devotion, paired with entertainment so mindless it borders on nihilism. Two opposite impulses holding hands across a basket of socks. That's not just my relationship. That's every civilization ever built by creatures who simultaneously wanted to make things perfect and also zone out completely.\n\nI could go bigger. I could talk about cells dividing inside our bodies right now, each one carrying instructions written three billion years ago\u2014and isn't that just the plot of every family dinner you've ever sat through?\n\nHere's the thing nobody tells you about microcosms: they don't reveal truths. They multiply them. Every time you zoom into...\n\nDoes this resonate?\n\n#TheNineStitches #WritingWisdom #CreativeProcess #WritingPhilosophy #AuthorLife\n\nDrop a note in the comments if this sounds familiar.",
